1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,286
If you've been watching AI coding tool announcements lately, you've heard the

2
00:00:04,286 --> 00:00:08,571
pitch: let agents handle your pull requests, automate your code review, ship

3
00:00:08,571 --> 00:00:12,857
faster. What's said less often is that the infrastructure behind those workflows

4
00:00:12,857 --> 00:00:17,143
is still being redesigned, the permission controls have known gaps, and the

5
00:00:17,143 --> 00:00:21,429
impressive scale numbers describe provider workloads—not yours. Here's what's been reported, what's

6
00:00:21,429 --> 00:00:25,000
still being built, and what stays your responsibility either way.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,776
Start with the numbers. GitHub reports that developers and agents made 7.38

8
00:00:29,776 --> 00:00:34,551
billion commits on the platform in September 2026—described by GitHub as more

9
00:00:34,551 --> 00:00:39,327
than five times the level from a year earlier—and that monthly pushes

10
00:00:39,327 --> 00:00:44,102
grew from roughly 0.69 billion to 3.35 billion year over year, which

11
00:00:44,102 --> 00:00:48,878
GitHub attributes substantially to agent-driven workloads. But GitHub is explicit: the repositories

12
00:00:48,878 --> 00:00:53,653
driving those figures are large engineering teams running dense CI pipelines alongside

13
00:00:53,653 --> 00:00:58,429
agent fleets. Large CI workloads and concentrated repository activity are alternative explanations

14
00:00:58,429 --> 00:01:03,204
for the growth. These figures do not measure what a student team

15
00:01:03,204 --> 00:01:04,000
would gain.

16
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:09,120
Anthropic's account—relayed through a commentary post by Simon Willison, not the primary

17
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:14,240
engineering article—shows a similar internal pattern. Daily code merges grew from roughly

18
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:19,360
2,500 in May 2026 to over 30,000 in September 2026, and Anthropic

19
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:24,480
built and migrated to a custom source-control system called Scion, with Claude

20
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:29,600
described as its heaviest user. This is infrastructure for an AI lab's

21
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:34,720
own agent workload. It is not externally available and was explicitly designed

22
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:36,000
without offline support.

23
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,235
Now look at what's available to you. OpenAI's Codex product video demonstrates

24
00:01:40,235 --> 00:01:44,471
a code-review workflow where an agent takes an automatic first-pass review of

25
00:01:44,471 --> 00:01:48,706
GitHub pull requests once repository permissions are configured. GitHub integration is described

26
00:01:48,706 --> 00:01:52,941
as generally available; GitLab is in preview. What the video does not

27
00:01:52,941 --> 00:01:57,176
provide is any evaluation of review accuracy, false-positive rates, or how often

28
00:01:57,176 --> 00:02:01,412
the agent misses something. That gap matters directly if you're deciding whether

29
00:02:01,412 --> 00:02:05,647
to treat the first pass as a genuine check or just a

30
00:02:05,647 --> 00:02:06,000
hint.

31
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,667
On permissions, an Anthropic employee on the Latent Space podcast described multiple

32
00:02:10,667 --> 00:02:15,333
layers being added to manage agent permissions at scale: inference-time probes and

33
00:02:15,333 --> 00:02:20,000
classifiers, an "auto mode" that checks whether agent actions match what the

34
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:24,667
user actually granted, and identity isolation for multi-agent contexts. One example given:

35
00:02:24,667 --> 00:02:29,333
an agent with database access might use computer-use capabilities to issue its

36
00:02:29,333 --> 00:02:34,000
own credentials and edit data—auto mode is designed to catch this by

37
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,667
comparing intent against granted scope. But the same account describes this as

38
00:02:38,667 --> 00:02:43,333
ongoing work with known edge cases, not a completed system. This is

39
00:02:43,333 --> 00:02:48,000
a vendor employee describing their own product, not an independent security audit.

40
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,642
Here is the distinction worth holding onto. GitHub states its planned new

41
00:02:52,642 --> 00:02:57,283
architecture must preserve branch protections, required reviews, and audit logs. GitHub also

42
00:02:57,283 --> 00:03:01,925
reports internal benchmarks showing up to 35 times higher write throughput—a figure

43
00:03:01,925 --> 00:03:06,566
from GitHub's own engineering team, not an independent evaluator. Willison's commentary adds

44
00:03:06,566 --> 00:03:11,208
something at a different layer: Anthropic used adversarial AI review while building

45
00:03:11,208 --> 00:03:15,849
Scion and found it effective but myopic—skilled engineers had to steer the

46
00:03:15,849 --> 00:03:20,491
model away from over-engineering in response to hypothetical concerns. One layer enforces

47
00:03:20,491 --> 00:03:25,132
workflow controls. The other requires human judgment about what the agent is

48
00:03:25,132 --> 00:03:29,000
actually optimizing for. Higher throughput does not replace either one.

49
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,786
One final note. The evidence relies heavily on provider accounts, some relayed

50
00:03:33,786 --> 00:03:38,571
through independent commentary. No small-team perspective, no unsuccessful deployment, no independent accuracy

51
00:03:38,571 --> 00:03:43,357
measurement appears in this evidence set. That absence matters. The practical question

52
00:03:43,357 --> 00:03:48,142
is not whether Anthropic's commit volume grew twelve-fold. It is: when an

53
00:03:48,142 --> 00:03:52,928
agent takes a first-pass review of your pull request, what should you

54
00:03:52,928 --> 00:03:57,713
still check—and how would you know if it missed something important? These

55
00:03:57,713 --> 00:04:02,100
sources do not establish how much review your own project needs.
